<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter6_fixed-size-arrays">
    <title>Fixed-Size Arrays</title>

    <p>
        A program often needs to process several values of the same type. A
        fixed-size array stores those values under one name. In this chapter, we
        use the standard-library type <c>std::array</c>.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_create_std_array"
             language="cpp"
             interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    for (int score : scores) {
        std::cout << score << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        The type <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;</c> means that <c>scores</c> stores
        five <c>int</c> values. Its size is fixed when the array is created. The
        member function <c>size()</c> reports the number of elements.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_fixed-size-arrays_range-based-loop">
        <title>Range-Based <c>for</c> Loops</title>
        <p>
            The phrase <q>for each score</q> describes a range-based
            <c>for</c> loop. The loop visits each element in order, so a separate
            index is not needed when only the value matters.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_fixed-size-arrays_indexing">
        <title>Accessing Elements by Index</title>
        <p>
            Each array element has an <term>index</term>, which identifies its
            position. The first element has index 0, not index 1. Indexed
            iteration is useful when a program needs to work with a position or
            compare neighboring elements.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_index_std_array"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << "Score " << index + 1 << ": "
                  << scores[index] << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Notice the difference between the array index and the displayed
            score number. The index begins at 0, while the human-friendly label
            begins at 1.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_fixed-size-arrays_search-summarize">
        <title>Searching and Summarizing Values</title>
        <p>
            A loop can inspect every element to search for a value, count values
            that meet a condition, or calculate a total. These tasks combine
            iteration with the counters and accumulators from the previous
            section.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_summarize_scores"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};
    int total = 0;
    int high_score_count = 0;

    for (int score : scores) {
        total += score;

        if (score >= 90) {
            ++high_score_count;
        }
    }

    std::cout << "Total: " << total << '\n';
    std::cout << "High scores: " << high_score_count << '\n';
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>
</section>
